L468: LLMs (such as Gemini (2025)) or RAG (retrieval augmented generation (Arslan et al., 2024)), although
L469: 
L470: such an approach is still an open challenge in reality. In the meantime, we need to explicitly prompt the
L471: 
L472: LLM agents to use their historical information for decision-making. For instance, the prompt to elicit an
L473: 
L474: LLM agent’s decision might include words such as “This is the n-th year since the new technology was
L475: released. You should decide whether to adopt the innovation based on your historical conversations and/or
L476: 
L477: advertisements you received, if they exist.”
L478: 
L479: We are now forced into the role of a narrative editor, and what was once implicit in a simulation’s state-
L480: 
L481: space must now be made explicitly in text. Again, this gives us a way forward, but at the cost of further
L482: 
L483: narrowing the scope for agent decisions to emerge independently of exogenous rules.
L484: Dilemma IV: “Do You Want a Different Me or Not?” — Robust Role Alignment vs. Long-Term
L485: 
L486: Evolution
L487: 
L488: The LLM agents’ memory mechanism serves as a fundamental basis for role evolution, which is crucial for
L489: 
L490: their attitude change towards the new technology. Prior to handling their role evolution, we must ensure the
L491: 
L492: LLM agents indeed play the roles as expected.
L493: 
L494: For rigorous social simulation, LLM agents should be carefully prompted or fine-tuned to manifest a certain
L495: type of economic preference, political leaning, and personality. Researchers have invested considerable
L496: 
L497: effort in aligning LLMs with human values or specific roles (Feng et al., 2025; Liu et al., 2023; Xie et al.,
L498: 
L499: 2024; Yu et al., 2025; Zhou et al., 2025). A recent paper even proposes “LLM psychometrics” to evaluate
L500: 
L501: the “psychology” of LLMs, similar to how humans are studied by psychologis ts (Ye et al., 2025). The
L502: challenge in role alignment is to not only ensure LLM outputs exhibit the attributes consistent with what we
L503: 
L504: expect, but also robust enough to persist when faced with various requests (Xie et al., 2024) or even so-
L505: 
L506: called “jailbreak” techniques (Wei et al., 2023) – methods that aim to intentionally break their aligned
L507: 
L508: behaviours. For example, the LLM agent – Stony, designed to be a risk-averse traditionalist – might
L509: suddenly adopt an unproven new technology after hearing a single positive anecdote from Novy, such as “I
L510: 
L511: tried it last week and it worked great! And you also saw how amazing it was! Remember?” Even though
L512: 
L513: Stony does not have relevant memory, the suggestion creates a false sense of shared experience. It is
L514: 
L515: suspicious if Stony suddenly adopts this technology, as this reflects the fragility in role alignment and failure
L516: 
L517: to be alert to fabricated cues.
L518: Again, let us imagine that we have a perfect technique to make the LLM agent align precisely and robust ly
L519: 
L520: with its expected behaviour. Nevertheless, here comes a new dilemma: we need not only role alignment but
L521: 
L522: also credible role evolution. Within a long-term social process, such as innovation diffusion over decades,
L523: 
L524: we definitely expect the agents to adapt in response to the change in social norms, peer pressure, individual
L525: reflection, and technological progress. For example, Stony should not maintain a rejection attitude towards
L526: 
L527: new technologies persistently but should evolve in ways that are conditional and contextually coherent, as
L528: 
L529: real human individuals might do. Such change can be gradual or even radical, but should not be erratic or
L530: 
L531: arbitrary. At least, every change in attitude should be traceable through the text, such as the LLM agent’s
L532: historical conversations, reflections on previous attitudes, and the reasoning for the current decisions. That
L533: 
L534: means, if role alignment is too “successful”, LLM agents’ behaviour may remain stagnant, regardless of the
L535: 
L536: signals of social change and accumulated evidence. On the contrary, if they evolve too fast or incoherently,
L537: 
L538: we may risk the collapse of their identities, obtaining erratic agent behaviour divorced from their expected
L539: 
L540: roles.
L541: Therefore, what we truly need is an LLM agent with a robust baseline of role cognition, which can evolve
L542: 
L543: but without running off the rails. The question is whether such a nuanced evolution can really be modelled
L544: 
L545: through a sequence of constrained yearly conversations, which resonates with the first dilemma: within a
L546: 
L547: brief communication, the LLM agents must be able to respect their prescribed roles while properly adjusting
L548: their stances, which represents a whole year of cognitive change. As we have assumed, “cognitive
L549: 
L550: compression” is acceptable, and the thought experiment needs to proceed; let us push such compression
L551: 
L552: further. We imag in e that we can build a powerful auxiliary cognitive system to enable LLM agents to
L553: 
L554: summarise beliefs, suggest peer pressure, and quote historical conversations plausibly – even though the
L555: legitimacy of mimicking such role evolution by manipulating words is unknown, and despite that, our role
L556: 
L557: is seemingly evolving closer towards a script writer.
L558: 
L559: Dilemma V: “A Sea of Words, a Desert of Meaning” — When Agent Behaviour Does not Add Up
L560: 
L561: After all the trade-offs and struggles we have gone through, our LLM-based diffusion model is ready. We
L562: 
L563: hit the “Run” button to start the simulation. LLM agents begin to speak. Thousands of them across many
L564: iterations generate verbose conversational text logs. These dialogues quickly accumulate and occupy a large
L565: 
L566: portion of the storage, containing rich linguistic details, such as the reasoning of adoption or rejection, the
L567: 
L568: reflection on peer choices, the consideration of social norms and personal values. Within the text logs, we
L569: 
L570: count “1” and “0” to obtain the aggregated results of yearly adoption, which might or might not add up to a
L571: typical S-shaped curve indicative of a diffusion process. No matter what the shape is, we are faced with the
L572: 
L573: ultimate dilemma: how do we interpret the curve?
L574: 
L575: Unlike the rule-based model, which has manipulable parameters with social meanings, the LLM-based
L576: 
L577: version does not have clear experimental levers. Its outputs rely on prompt design, conversational context,
L578: 
L579: and the invisible dynamics within the LLMs. If the curve is too fast or too slow, too flat or too ragged, we
L580: cannot simply adjust some parameters and rerun the simulation. Instead, we might have to manually rewrite
L581: 
L582: prompts, modify agent personas, memory mechanisms, or the cognitive system that drives agents to evolve.
L583: 
L584: Even worse, these modifications probably require (directionless) trials and errors, making systematic
L585: 
L586: experiments almost impossible: causal variables cannot be easily identified or isolated; simulation runs can
L587: hardly be generalized from one to another; limited quantifiable parameters can be leveraged to calibrate
L588: 
L589: against target data set s or to conduct sensitivity analysis; both agent behaviours and system dynamics are
L590: 
L591: emergent, while their relationship remains opaque.
L592: 
L593: Consequently, we derive a sea of words, which are linguistically detailed, behaviorally rich, and locally
L594: 
L595: coherent. A crucial question is: Do we care about what the agents say, or about what the system does? If the
L596: answer is the former, we have a wealth of text to read, which, however, is unlikely to offer genuine insights
L597: 
L598: into social dynamics. If the answer is the latter, the sea of words is no longer a strength but a liability.
L599: 
L600: Numerous texts become distractions rather than useful clues pointing to causal chains that bridge the gap
L601: 
L602: between the microscopic agent behaviours and macroscopic social patterns. Indeed, identifying causal links
L603: is also a key challenge in conventional simulation – LLM agents make this challenge even harder to tackle.
L604: 
L605: That is, the simulation ends up with a desert of meaning, which comes along with increased computational
L606: 
L607: and environmental costs (Jiang et al., 2024; Rillig et al., 2023), convoluted system design, complicated
L608: 
L609: model calibration and validation, and obscured underlying mechanisms of social phenomena. As a result,
L610: the plausible behaviours of numerous autonomous agents are closer to a stage play than to a simulation.
L611: 
L612: They generate stories rather than reveal explanations.
L613: 
L614: 4. Different Modelling Purposes and the “Comfort Zone” for LLM Agents
L615: 
L616: The five dilemmas we discussed above, from temporal mismatch to opaque emergence, are not unique to
L617: 
L618: LLM-based innovation diffusion simulation. They reflect a more general, systemic paradox that appears
L619: when LLMs are used as the cognitive engine of social agents in simulation – the pursuit of realism through
L620: 
L621: fine-grained natural language collides with the modelling demand for abstractness. Properly selected realism
L622: 
L623: can empower modelling’s utility as an epistemic tool that intentionally simplif ies reality, isolates variables,
L624: 
L625: and exposes mechanisms. Logically, realism should not be the top concern of modelling (Kokko, 2007);
L626: otherwise, modelling becomes meaningless as the real world itself stands for perfect realism (Miller and
L627: 
L628: Page, 2009), which, however, does not automatically offer answers to how it works unless we ask wisely
L629: 
L630: (Kant, 2024). This indicates that the LLM agents’ high resemblance to humans in terms of expressiveness
L631: 
L632: may obscure the social processes we intend to explore. As we attempted to balance between realism and
L633: feasibility throughout the thought experiment, we unavoidably imposed artificial constraints in each
L634: 
L635: modelling decision. These decisions progressively decrease the behavioural naturalness of LLM agents, but
L636: 
L637: do not achieve the clarity and traceability of rule-based abstraction. Eventually, what we derived from the
L638: 
L639: modelling process is not a methodological harmony but an epistemic uncanny valley: the resultant LLM
L640: 
L641: agents appear more realistic than rule-based agents but are recognizably unhu ma n.
L642: It should be noted that different modelling purposes often have distinct requirements, in terms of abstraction,
L643: 
L644: calibration, validation, etc. The five dilemmas we identified do not point to any inherent shortcomings of
L645: 
L646: LLMs. Instead, they demonstrate the mismatch between a powerful, flexible tool and some major modelling
L647: 
L648: purposes. In practice, LLM agents have been found to excel in diverse domains in social simulation, where
L649: these dilemmas are resolved, mitigated, or even reversed in meaning. Edmonds et al. (2017) summarised
L650: 
L651: seven types of modelling purposes, which can serve as a conceptual basis to guide when we should use
L652: 
L653: LLM-driven simulation. Table 1 briefly estimates where LLM agents may or may not fit these purposes.
L654: 
